# paste — real-world examples

## Concatenate columns from files

```sh
paste file1 file2 ...
```

## List files in the current directory in three columns

```sh
ls | paste - - -
```

## Combine pairs of lines from a file into single lines

```sh
paste -s -d '\t\n' myfile
```

## Number the lines in a file, similar to nl(1)

```sh
sed = myfile | paste -s -d '\t\n' - -
```

## Build a colon-separated PATH-style list

```sh
find / -name bin -type d | paste -s -d : -
```

## Homelab: join a hostname list and an IP list into a CSV

```sh
paste -d, hosts.txt ips.txt > inventory.csv
# homelab,192.168.1.42
# mediaserver,192.168.1.50
```

| Flag | Meaning                                        |
| ---- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| -d   | delimiter list (cycled through, default tab)   |
| -s   | serial: one file at a time, lines joined       |
